A||||13||black-86-GT||crazy oil pressure gauge||||1||
Z||000000||black-86-GT||07-05-2000||10:52 PM||||I was wondering if any of you have ever had this problem before. at idle the oil pressure gauge pegs out all the way then if you rev up the engine or drive normally it goes back to normal. not really a problem but would like to get it fixed just one of those things||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bruce||07-06-2000||12:38 AM||||Mine, and about everyone else's, does the same thing. Not a big deal.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||07-06-2000||02:29 AM||||Mine did that for awhile, then it would stay pegged all the time.  That's when I noticed the fuel pump was on all the time also.<BR>A new sender fixed it for about five months then started doing it again, the whole routine including the constantly on fuel pump when it pegged forever.<P>Another new sender AND a new sender harness has corrected the problem for three years now without a single wild needle.<P>Don't like changing parts without knowing why,  The sender sits upright in a high vibration, wet and dirty area so I blamed corrosion through the harness weatherseal.<BR>But that seems lame as these same parts are used in hundreds of thousands of vehicles.||||reg||12
Z||000003||Taijiguy||07-06-2000||08:03 AM||||Glad to know I'm not the only one who experienced that! I thought I had a major oil pressure problem going on. My GT normally runs almost 80 psi as it is, which is at the top of the scale, but then sitting at idle, watching that needle blast off the scale is a little freaky. I attributed it to a short, but could never isolate it. <P>Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||07-06-2000||09:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phaeton:<BR><B>Mine did that for awhile, then it would stay pegged all the time.  That's when I noticed the fuel pump was on all the time also.<BR>A new sender fixed it for about five months then started doing it again, the whole routine including the constantly on fuel pump when it pegged forever.<P>Another new sender AND a new sender harness has corrected the problem for three years now without a single wild needle.<P>Don't like changing parts without knowing why,  The sender sits upright in a high vibration, wet and dirty area so I blamed corrosion through the harness weatherseal.<BR>But that seems lame as these same parts are used in hundreds of thousands of vehicles.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sending unit for what?  Oil pressure or something to do with the fuel pump? <P><P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||07-06-2000||05:30 PM||||Both.  On the V6, the oil pressure sending unit has a switch in it that turns on the fuel pump if there's oil pressure.<P>There are 3 terminals on the sending unit.  One terminal sends the signal to the oil pressure gage.  The other two, if you connect them together, will send juice to the electric fuel pump - even if ignition switch is off.<P>Sorry if I'm telling you something you already knew  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> !   ||||reg||6
Z||000006||Monkeyman||07-07-2000||10:15 AM||||Didn't know.  Are '85s set up the same since they didn't have the aux. gauges option?<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||07-07-2000||02:52 PM||||Not absolutely sure, since I never owned a 1985 model.   However, if your '85 has an oil pressure gage (in the tach, maybe?), and if your oil pressure sender is a three-terminal job, located on a bracket on RHS of car,  near the battery box,  I'd bet it works the same way I just described.<P>(I think) the center terminal controls the oil pressure gage;  outside two terminals are the two you connect together to make fuel pump run.  ||||reg||5
Z||000008||Monkeyman||07-08-2000||09:25 AM||||I <I>had</I> the stock '85 GT gauges, but swapped them out for some '87 GT gauges when I installed the aux gauges.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000009||PJ||07-08-2000||09:54 AM||||When my '88GT was new, I went through about 4 senders the first year I had it. When it went out of warranty I checked the TSB's and found one for relocating the sender to the boss on the block next to the oil filter. Of course the dealer swore he never heard of the bulletin so I ordered a copy and made them do it. Workin' fine now for the past 11 years. I'll dig the TSB out if anyone's interested.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||07-08-2000||10:24 AM||||Yes I'd like to know what the TSB # is, or if you can scan in a copy.<BR>Thanks<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Bruce||07-08-2000||11:13 AM||||Duh, what's "TSB" stand for?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Getaway2k||07-08-2000||04:58 PM||||I think it stands for Technical Service Bulletin. My 86 GT has the same retarded oil pressure guage. See if your guage operates at a normal rate when you are taching at 2000 rpms (or greater). If so, it's a good indicator of a bad ground wire.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||PJ||07-09-2000||11:05 AM||||The bulletin # is 88-8-22.<P>Basically on '85-'87 models (2.8 w/AC) it says to add a shield (p/n 10096127) to protect against water intrusion. (Sounds like a really lame band-aid to me.) <P>On '88's the procedure is to relocate the sender under the A/C compressor. You remove the mounting tube and throw it away. Fashion a small length of brass pipe and angles to run from the 1/8" pipe female fitting on the block by the oil filter to where you want the sender to live. The sender is 1/4" so you need a reducer in the run somewhere. <P>Mine is a short "L" shaped run where the short leg (&gt;1") goes into the block. The long leg (~2") runs horizontally towards the front of the block and ends with a 90 degree angle. The sender screws into the angle and points straight down. As for the wiring, you pull it out of the conduit far enough so you can double it back to where it needs to go and tape it (or ty-rap) to the main harness to secure it. A big dollop of RTV on the wire side of the connector will help keep the water out. All-in-all a pretty simple procedure and it does seem to work! I haven't had to replace the sender since (11+ yrs).<P>I don't have access to a scanner at the moment but I can fax the bulletin to anyone who wants a copy. Just E-Mail me your fax #.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>pjsolo@bellsouth.net||||reg||1
